{
  "id": "tak-5x5",
  "name": "Tak (5×5)",
  "description": "Build an unbroken road of your stones from one side of the board to the opposite side. Stacks, walls and a capstone make it a game of tempo and tactics.",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "designers": ["James Ernest", "Patrick Rothfuss"],
  "publisher": "Cheapass Games",
  "rules": [
    "Goal: connect two opposite edges of the board with an orthogonally connected chain of your flat stones and capstone (a \"road\"). If nobody manages that, the game ends when one player has no stones left in hand or the board is full, and the player with more flat stones on top of stacks wins.",
    "Turn order: White moves first. On each player's first turn they must place a flat stone of the OPPONENT's color on any empty square. After that, players alternate; on your turn do exactly one of: place a stone from your hand, or move a stack you control.",
    "Placing: put one stone from your hand on an empty square. It can be a flat stone (lies flat, counts for roads and flat count), a standing stone or wall (blocks movement, counts for neither roads nor flat count) or your capstone (counts for roads, can't be covered, and can flatten walls).",
    "Moving: you control a stack if your piece is on top. Pick up any number of pieces from the top of a stack (at most the board size), then move in a straight line without turning back, dropping at least one piece on each square you enter. Pieces are dropped bottom-first. You may not move onto a wall or capstone, except that a capstone moving alone onto a wall as the last drop of its move flattens the wall into a flat stone.",
    "Roads: only flat stones and capstones on TOP of a square count. If one move gives both players a road, the player who moved wins."
  ],
  "preview": [
